<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  Cleartext is permitted globally, and that is a deliberate, uncomfortable choice. It is written
  down here rather than left to be discovered, because the previous version of this comment claimed
  the opposite of what the file does.

  Why it cannot be narrowed: peer-to-peer sync fetches audio straight off another phone on the same
  LAN, over plain HTTP to whatever address DHCP handed that phone (see `OffGridTransport`,
  `AgroUploader`, `ListenAlongResolver`). Android resolves this policy from static XML at build
  time and supports neither CIDR ranges nor wildcards, so there is no way to name "the local
  network" — only literal hosts, which are not knowable until a peer appears. A `base-config` of
  false turns P2P off entirely.

  What carries the security instead of TLS: the P2P audio payload is already end-to-end encrypted
  before it reaches the socket — ChaCha20-Poly1305 under a key sealed to the receiving peer's
  X25519 identity, so the bytes on the wire are readable by that peer and by nothing else on the
  network (see `P2PServer`'s sealed-stream path and `AudioStreamCipher`). Transport TLS on top of
  that would encrypt already-encrypted bytes; what it would add is protection for the small
  metadata routes (ping, pair, now-playing, unpair), and it would require a self-signed X.509
  identity per device plus certificate pinning at every call site, since peers are bare IPs with no
  hostname to verify.

  What this does NOT license: anything carrying a credential must still be HTTPS. A self-hosted
  Navidrome or Agro belongs behind a reverse proxy with a real certificate — the permissive base
  config here is not an invitation to point one at `http://`. Tokens, passwords and session cookies
  never travel over plain HTTP in this app, and that is a property of the call sites, not of this
  file.

  The domain-config below is now redundant with a permissive base and is kept only as
  documentation of the development loopbacks: `localhost`/`127.0.0.1` is the
  `adb reverse tcp:8700 tcp:8700` flow, and `10.0.2.2` is the emulator's route to its host.
-->
<network-security-config>
    <base-config cleartextTrafficPermitted="true">
        <trust-anchors>
            <certificates src="system" />
        </trust-anchors>
    </base-config>

    <domain-config cleartextTrafficPermitted="true">
        <domain includeSubdomains="true">localhost</domain>
        <domain includeSubdomains="true">127.0.0.1</domain>
        <domain includeSubdomains="true">10.0.2.2</domain>
    </domain-config>
</network-security-config>
